"""Original applications for the stock-ML lifecycle and algorithm evaluation."""

APPLICATIONS = [
    ("7.1", "ml_pipeline", "A stock-ML project needs a decision and evaluation horizon before a dataset or model is chosen.", "A fictional Indonesian-stock research team wants a weekly shortlist. Define the eligible universe, prediction target, decision time, holding horizon, comparison rule, and the output used by the analyst.", "A weekly ranking is different from a price forecast or an automatic trading instruction. Name the downstream decision and its limits so labels, validation, and metrics match the task. A lifecycle includes data correction, monitoring, and retirement as well as the first successful training run."),
    ("7.2", "edgar", "Source provenance determines whether a feature was both correct and available when a prediction was made.", "A fictional March prediction uses a December-period profitability figure published in April. Identify the leakage and specify an evidence record that would prevent the join.", "Store publication/availability time, measurement period, filing identifier, units, and revision version. Exclude the April release from the March information set and use the latest eligible older record. Fiscal-period labels and present-day downloads are insufficient proof of historical availability."),
    ("7.3", "negative_prices", "Data validation should express the intended stock dataset's contract while recognizing other products use different rules.", "A fictional cash-equity file contains duplicate stock-date rows, a negative close, and negative volume. Define rejection and quarantine behavior without applying a blanket rule to unrelated futures data.", "Enforce unique keys, finite positive equity prices, and nonnegative volume under the declared schema. Preserve rejected row identities and reasons before training. The CME example explains why instrument classification matters; it does not justify accepting a negative cash-equity close."),
    ("7.4", "bca_split", "A mechanical share transformation must not become an artificial crash label or an extreme model feature.", "A fictional stock's raw close drops from 50 to 10 on a five-for-one split. Explain how to distinguish this from a bad tick and how to update returns, share counts, and data lineage.", "Use verified corporate-action metadata and a consistent adjustment convention. The split alone produces no ownership loss. Preserve raw data and transformation history; blindly clipping the move as an outlier or imputing yesterday's price hides the cause and can corrupt later features."),
    ("7.5", "ml_pipeline", "A rolling feature's timestamp determines the earliest decision for which it is usable.", "A fictional stock ranker runs before Monday's open. Specify the eligible observations for a five-session trailing return and a rolling volume feature, including a holiday and a missing Friday record.", "Use only observations available before the decision and count trading sessions according to the feature contract. Define missing-record handling explicitly; do not reach into Monday's close to complete the window. Preserve feature timestamps and warm-up eligibility for both training and inference."),
    ("7.6", "calibration", "The label must describe the actual opportunity horizon and cost convention the model is meant to support.", "A fictional classifier predicts whether a next-open entry and fifth-session close exit beats round-trip cost of 0.4%. Define the positive label and what happens when either executable price is missing.", "A positive label requires the specified gross return to exceed 0.4% under the stated convention. Missing entry or exit prices need a declared exclusion or execution policy. Labeling from today's close instead would train for a different opportunity than the one the analyst can act on."),
    ("7.7", "ml_pipeline", "Randomly splitting stock rows can share both market conditions and overlapping outcomes across train and test.", "A fictional panel contains 50 stocks per date and ten-session forward labels. Design a chronological split that groups each date and purges training labels reaching into validation.", "Use date groups and actual label-end timestamps, keeping all learned selection within training/validation. Merely separating stock identifiers does not prevent future market information from entering training. Record the discarded boundary observations so another researcher can reproduce the eligible sample."),
    ("7.8", "ml_pipeline", "Imputation and scaling are learned model components that can leak future distribution information.", "A fictional feature's mean is 10 in training and 100 in a later test regime. Explain how filling all missing values with the full-sample mean contaminates evaluation, and specify the pipeline fix.", "Fit the imputer and scaler on training data inside each validation fold, then transform later rows with those fitted objects. Persist those transformations with the estimator. A distribution shift should appear as an evaluation challenge rather than silently influence the training-time fill value."),
    ("7.9", "ml_pipeline", "A regularized stock model needs a simple comparator to establish whether extra complexity helps.", "Compare a fictional ridge return model with zero-return and historical-mean baselines on the same future dates. Specify regression loss, tuning data, and a separate economic evaluation.", "Choose regularization through chronological validation and report prediction errors against both baselines. A small error improvement may be concentrated in untradable stocks or disappear after costs. Keep prediction quality and portfolio usefulness connected but measured separately."),
    ("7.10", "calibration", "High accuracy can be obtained by ignoring the rare outcome that motivated the classifier.", "In 1,000 fictional observations only 50 exceed a high-return threshold. Compute the accuracy of always predicting no event, then propose metrics that reveal whether a trained model adds value.", "The trivial classifier is 95% accurate while detecting none of the events. Inspect precision, recall, confusion counts, probability scores, and the cost of decisions at stated thresholds. Preserve class prevalence and dates in evaluation rather than manufacturing a balanced test distribution."),
    ("7.11", "ml_pipeline", "Comparing tree models fairly requires identical data, validation opportunities, and implementation assumptions.", "A fictional team compares a shallow tree, random forest, and boosted trees. Define a shared chronological evaluation and a limited tuning budget, then check whether liquidity dominates their predictions.", "Fit preprocessing only where appropriate inside each training fold and compare the same held-out rows. Record all tuning trials and feature ablations. A larger search budget can make one model look better through selection alone; an exposure proxy may explain apparent stock-selection skill."),
    ("7.12", "ml_pipeline", "Sequence models require enough independent history and precise alignment between windows and future targets.", "A fictional neural model consumes 60-session windows for 30 stocks. Explain why the number of overlapping windows overstates independent evidence and define a date-based evaluation with simple baselines.", "Neighboring windows share most inputs and may share outcomes, so row count is not effective sample size. Prevent boundary leakage, preserve feature order, and compare against simpler models with the same targets. Extra architecture complexity needs demonstrated future usefulness and reproducible training behavior."),
    ("7.13", "ml_pipeline", "Hyperparameter selection should be replayed as a process that knew only the history available at each origin.", "Design a fictional walk-forward search that chooses tree depth from three values using inner chronological folds and evaluates the selected pipeline on the next outer month.", "Repeat the inner search for each outer origin, freeze the selected pipeline, and save future predictions before outcomes mature. Report the whole process, including unstable choices. Selecting a single depth after inspecting all outer months would reuse evaluation information."),
    ("7.14", "calibration", "Different metrics can disagree because they measure different aspects of a stock forecast.", "A fictional model improves rank correlation but worsens probability loss and trades more often. Explain which measurements answer ranking, probability reliability, and practical portfolio usefulness.", "Rank correlation concerns ordering, proper probability scores concern probabilistic forecasts, and net portfolio evaluation includes costs and exposure. Report the relevant metrics together using matched dates. Do not choose whichever metric flatters the model after inspecting the results."),
    ("7.15", "calibration", "Decision thresholds should combine reliable probabilities with the asymmetric payoff of a trade.", "A fictional opportunity gains 2 on success, loses 3 otherwise, and costs 0.1 in either case. Calculate the break-even success probability and state how calibration uncertainty affects its use.", "Expected payoff is 5p − 3.1, so break-even is 0.62. A prediction of 0.65 only narrowly clears that assumption-dependent threshold. Check reliability on separate data and stress payoffs and costs before interpreting a small probability margin as actionable evidence."),
    ("7.16", "flash_crash", "Economic evaluation must account for the liquidity needed to turn a stock ranking into fills.", "A fictional top-ranked portfolio shows 0.6% gross weekly advantage but requires high turnover in thin stocks. Evaluate estimated cost of 0.4% and a stressed cost of 0.8% on the same convention.", "The simple net advantages are 0.2% and −0.2%, respectively. Include uncertainty, delayed fills, capacity, and concentrated exposures. A favorable point estimate under normal costs may not survive the conditions in which the model trades most aggressively."),
    ("7.17", "bca_split", "Error slices can reveal a data defect hidden by an acceptable aggregate model score.", "A fictional model's largest errors cluster on corporate-action dates and newly listed stocks. Design slice metrics and record-level checks before deciding to change the estimator.", "Check split adjustments, history requirements, labels, and source availability for those rows. Compare counts and uncertainty so a tiny slice is not overinterpreted. Correcting a data fault may be the right intervention; a more flexible model could merely learn to accommodate corrupted inputs."),
    ("7.18", "ml_pipeline", "Feature explanations can divide credit arbitrarily among correlated measurements of the same underlying exposure.", "A fictional model uses price momentum and a nearly identical lagged-return feature. Compare their individual importance with a grouped ablation and explain why neither establishes causality.", "Correlated features can substitute for each other, making individual importance unstable. Refit a controlled grouped ablation on eligible training data and evaluate future changes. The explanation describes model behavior under the chosen method; it does not show that changing the feature would cause returns to change."),
    ("7.19", "ldi", "A model selected in ordinary periods needs explicit uncertainty about behavior in rare stressed regimes.", "Evaluate a fictional stock model with spread doubling, volume halving, and a volatility jump. Keep parameters frozen and record whether previous tuning included the stressed period.", "Measure prediction, exposure, turnover, and executable net performance together. Stress assumptions explore vulnerability rather than forecast a specific crisis. Disclose the research trial history and limited number of observed stress episodes instead of treating one successful scenario as robust general proof."),
    ("7.20", "bca_split", "A data correction should trigger a traceable retraining cycle rather than an unexplained overwrite of a model file.", "A fictional team discovers that split adjustments were applied twice. Specify corrected data versioning, affected-label identification, controlled retraining, and comparison with the previous release.", "Preserve the faulty snapshot for diagnosis, create a corrected version, and regenerate dependent features and labels. Repeat the declared training process and compare on eligible evaluation data. Clearly label any previously inspected period; fixing data does not make observed test outcomes unknown again."),
    ("7.21", "model_release", "A deployable research model includes the transformations and environment needed to interpret incoming features.", "Package a fictional stock ranker with imputer, scaler, estimator, ordered schema, training dates, dependencies, artifact hash, and a model card. Define a known-input prediction check after loading.", "The loaded trusted artifact should reproduce saved predictions within the declared tolerance in a compatible environment. Store intended use and known limitations beside it. Avoid loading arbitrary untrusted serialized model files; persistence formats and dependencies have different compatibility and execution properties."),
    ("7.22", "model_release", "Shadow inference reveals feature and environment mismatches before predictions influence a paper decision.", "Run a fictional candidate model beside the approved pipeline on identical dated batches. Compare feature order, missingness, transformations, scores, and target positions without granting the candidate order authority.", "Investigate discrepancies at the earliest differing stage rather than only comparing final returns. Preserve batch and artifact IDs and define promotion criteria in advance. Shadow agreement establishes parity for tested inputs; it does not yet establish superior future economic performance."),
    ("7.23", "model_release", "A release decision needs separate evidence about current input health and eventually observable outcomes.", "A fictional five-session model receives a new vendor feed with changed volume units. Define immediate schema/distribution checks, mature-label monitoring, and a rollback condition tied to evidence.", "Detect and quarantine the unit change before trusting scores. Evaluate realized prediction quality only after the specified horizons mature, grouped by release and data version. A decline in a monitored metric should trigger diagnosis under the policy rather than automatic retraining that hides the source fault."),
    ("7.24", "reporting", "A lifecycle review should show the complete chain from dated evidence to a monitored, reversible paper release.", "Prepare a fictional stock-ML dossier containing provenance, validation splits, baseline comparisons, selected trials, error slices, cost stress, model package, shadow results, and a conditional release recommendation.", "Link each decision to reproducible artifacts and disclose what failed or remains untested. Explain whether the model merits more research, restricted paper use, or rejection. The final claim should match demonstrated evidence and the stated use case rather than the most favorable isolated metric."),

    ("10.1", "reporting", "An evaluation contract prevents the success criterion from moving after results are visible.", "Before running a fictional strategy's final period, write the benchmark, sample, cost convention, primary metric, uncertainty method, acceptable operational limits, and the decision each result will support.", "Version the contract with the frozen code and data plan. Later changes are possible but must be disclosed as a revised or exploratory analysis. A report is easier to assess when the reader can distinguish prespecified criteria from explanations developed after the outcome."),
    ("10.2", "atomic_ledger", "Python and C outputs need a shared schema before their performance summaries can be reconciled.", "Specify fictional export columns for timestamp, cash, shares, price, fees, equity, and event ID. Include units, timezone, missing-value policy, and numeric tolerances for comparing both engines.", "Compare identical event fixtures and locate the first differing state, rather than comparing only rounded final returns. Export enough detail to explain each transition. A consistent schema makes a disagreement traceable to data, arithmetic, execution timing, or an explicitly different convention."),
    ("10.3", "factors", "A meaningful benchmark should reflect the market and opportunity set the algorithm was allowed to use.", "A fictional Indonesian small-stock strategy is compared with a U.S. large-stock price index. Identify mismatches and propose a dated local comparator with a consistent currency and dividend convention.", "Align geography, investable universe, currency, calendar, and total-return treatment before assessing relative results. Add exposure-aware comparisons where justified. The international factor source supplies methodological definitions, not evidence that a mismatched benchmark is appropriate for the local strategy."),
    ("10.4", "household", "Depositing more cash increases account value without being an investment gain.", "A fictional account starts at 100, earns 10% before a period-end contribution of 50, and finishes at 160 immediately after that contribution. Separate investment growth from the external cash flow.", "Investment growth is 10 and the contribution is 50; the pre-flow return is 10%. Treating the rise from 100 to 160 as a 60% return would be wrong. Record flow timing and choose a stated time-weighted or money-weighted convention for longer histories."),
    ("10.5", "reporting", "Compounding can turn offsetting percentage moves into a loss even when their arithmetic mean is zero.", "A fictional strategy earns 10% and then loses 10% over two periods. Compute cumulative return and explain what extra information is required before annualizing it.", "Wealth is multiplied by 1.1×0.9 = 0.99, giving a 1% loss. The arithmetic average is zero but does not describe compounded growth. Annualization also requires period frequency, duration, and a convention that is sensible for the observed sample."),
    ("10.6", "reporting", "An undefined risk ratio should remain visible instead of being converted into an attractive score.", "A fictional daily excess-return series is constant, so its sample standard deviation is zero. Specify how the report should display a Sharpe ratio and what diagnostics should accompany it.", "The ratio has a zero denominator and should be reported as undefined with an explanation. Inspect sample length, stale marks, rounding, and return construction. Do not replace it with an arbitrary huge value or silently remove the row from a comparison table."),
    ("10.7", "household", "Drawdown duration matters to a saver who may need money before the account recovers.", "A fictional equity path is 100, 120, 90, 108, and 121 at equally spaced dates. Find maximum drawdown and identify peak, trough, and recovery observations.", "Maximum drawdown is 25%, from the second observation's peak of 120 to the third observation's 90. The prior peak is first exceeded at the fifth observation. State the observation frequency before translating that interval into days or months, and distinguish depth from time underwater."),
    ("10.8", "flash_crash", "Execution sensitivity shows whether a reported edge survives a less favorable but plausible market environment.", "A fictional strategy earns gross profit 1,200 with fees 200 and slippage 300. Recompute net profit if slippage doubles, and identify omitted capacity and unfilled-order costs.", "Base net profit is 700 and stressed net profit is 400. This arithmetic isolates one assumption, not every implementation risk. Report turnover, participation, missing fills, and stressed liquidity so the reader can judge whether the cost model matches the strategy's required trading."),
    ("10.9", "ml_pipeline", "A final report should distinguish fitting, selection, and future evaluation periods on one dated timeline.", "Draw a fictional walk-forward timeline with training, validation, outer evaluation, and overlapping trade-label intervals. Mark where preprocessing is fitted and which observations are purged.", "Show that every reported future prediction came from a pipeline selected using earlier eligible information. Save fold membership and label-end timestamps as artifacts. A single train/test percentage is insufficient to audit chronology when observations and outcomes overlap."),
    ("10.10", "reporting", "Uncertainty around a chosen algorithm includes the fact that it won a research competition.", "A fictional analyst selects the best of 80 variants and reports a conventional confidence interval for that winner. Explain the missing selection context and propose a more transparent evidence package.", "Disclose the full trial ledger, selection rule, paired baseline comparisons, dependence-aware uncertainty, and any independent later evaluation. A conditional interval for one fixed rule does not account for searching 80 alternatives. Avoid suggesting that a bootstrap alone automatically solves every form of selection bias."),
    ("10.11", "ldi", "Robustness asks whether conclusions survive nearby choices and interacting stresses rather than one favorable setting.", "A fictional strategy's best lookback is 37 days. Compare nearby windows and a combined volatility, liquidity, and collateral stress, keeping the original selection record visible.", "Report the shape of performance across the neighborhood instead of only the maximum. Stress may create forced execution that an ordinary return simulation omits. Stability supports a narrower claim about tested conditions; it does not establish universal robustness or a guaranteed crisis response."),
    ("10.12", "gpu", "Computing performance belongs beside trading results when it changes the feasibility of a research or decision workflow.", "A fictional GPU version cuts kernel time from 10 to 2 ms but adds 12 ms of transfer and setup, while the CPU job totals 11 ms. Compare end-to-end latency and state the benchmark scope.", "The GPU path totals 14 ms and is slower for this measured workload. Report output parity, hardware, batch size, repeated timing distributions, and initialization policy. A faster kernel can still be useful at larger batches, but that requires a separate measured claim."),
    ("10.13", "reporting", "Clear charts expose the comparison and limitations instead of making selected results look more certain than they are.", "Design a fictional result table and equity chart with strategy and benchmark, identical dates, gross/net distinction, drawdown, sample count, and uncertainty. Decide how to display missing or undefined metrics.", "Label axes, units, costs, and rebasing, retain unfavorable periods, and mark unavailable values explicitly. Link chart data to reproducible exports. Avoid mixing a price-only benchmark with net total-return strategy results or using selective start dates that obscure the declared evaluation window."),
    ("10.14", "factors", "Attribution should explain a return difference without converting an accounting decomposition into a causal claim.", "A fictional strategy beats its benchmark by 4 percentage points. A stated model attributes 3 points to market/sector exposure and leaves 1 unexplained. Draft a precise interpretation and a sensitivity check.", "Under the chosen model, most relative return is associated with common exposures and one point remains residual. It is not automatically skill or a causal effect. Test justified alternative exposure definitions and report uncertainty, timing, and omitted-factor limitations."),
    ("10.15", "reporting", "An executive conclusion should connect evidence to a bounded decision a reader can act on.", "A fictional algorithm beats its baseline after normal costs but fails a liquidity stress and has only six months of future evaluation. Write a recommendation with one next check and a condition for rejection.", "A defensible conclusion may support further restricted paper evaluation while withholding broader deployment. Name the failed liquidity assumption, short history, and next capacity test. Avoid letting a positive average return bury the limitations that determine whether the strategy can actually be used."),
    ("10.16", "reporting", "The final report is complete when its headline claims can be rebuilt and its decision can be challenged.", "Deliver a fictional evaluation bundle with the frozen contract, data/code manifests, event exports, benchmark alignment, metric tables, charts, trial history, stress results, and balanced conclusion.", "Check every headline against a reproducible artifact and ensure the conclusion reflects both favorable and adverse evidence. Include exact reproduction instructions and known gaps. The cited reporting principles guide clarity and disclosure; completing this course assignment is not a claim of GIPS compliance."),
]
